ntdll: an opt-in quirk that grows a commit a write runs off the end of.

OFF unless WINE_GROW_COMMIT_ON_FAULT is set, and it must stay off by default:
Windows faults here too, so a program that walks off the end of a commit on
purpose -- to find where it ends -- would silently get different behaviour. This
exists to answer a question, and to make one game playable while that question
is open. It is not a claim that the fault is wrong.

THE QUESTION. Resident Evil Requiem dies in a stock MSVC memmove, in the same
place every run: a 240-byte copy to 0x22F42F20 whose last 32-byte AVX store
crosses into 0x22F43000, which is not committed. patches/wine/0045 measured the
allocation and the guest asked for 0x3000 and received exactly 0x3000, so
nothing below the guest shorted it, and a full audit of this file found no path
that could. What is left is the guest's own arithmetic:

    0x2F20 + 0xE0 = 0x3000    what the game committed
    0x2F20 + 0xF0 = 0x3010    what memmove writes

It sized the commit for a 224-byte object and then copied 240. The same title is
reported to run on other Android Wine stacks, so this is not a game that simply
always overruns -- something here differs and has not been found yet.

WHAT IT BUYS. Give it the sixteen bytes and watch. If the game proceeds, every
layer below the guest is sound and the overrun is benign on the machines it
ships on. If it dies elsewhere, the overrun was a symptom and the real
divergence is upstream.

NARROW ON PURPOSE. Only a write; only when no view covers the faulting page at
all; and only when the page immediately before it is the last page of a
committed view -- the shape of a copy running off the end of an allocation,
rather than a wild pointer into unmapped space. One page, never more, and the
growth is logged every time it happens.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -4974,6 +4974,22 @@
 /***********************************************************************
  *           virtual_handle_fault
  */
+/* Vessel: see the quirk in virtual_handle_fault. Read once, because a fault
+ * handler is not a place to call getenv. */
+static int grow_commit_on_fault = -1;
+
+static BOOL want_grow_commit_on_fault(void)
+{
+    if (grow_commit_on_fault == -1)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "WINE_GROW_COMMIT_ON_FAULT" );
+        grow_commit_on_fault = (e && *e && *e != '0');
+        if (grow_commit_on_fault)
+            ERR( "WINE_GROW_COMMIT_ON_FAULT is set: a write one page past a commit will grow it\n" );
+    }
+    return grow_commit_on_fault;
+}
+
 NTSTATUS virtual_handle_fault( EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec, void *stack )
 {
     NTSTATUS ret = STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION;
@@ -5028,6 +5044,60 @@
                 ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
         }
     }
